---
layout: article
title: Videos
description: Learn how an Appwrite video points at a Storage file, which metadata it holds, how its status is derived, and what deleting it removes.
---

A **video** is the root resource in Appwrite Videos. It points at a video or audio file in a [Storage](/docs/products/storage) bucket and holds the media metadata probed from that file. [Renditions](/docs/products/videos/renditions), [captions](/docs/products/videos/captions), and the [timeline](/docs/products/videos/timeline) all belong to a video.

# Source file {% #source-file %}

You create a video from a `bucketId` and a `fileId`. The file must be a video or audio file, such as MP4, MOV, WebM, MKV, MP3, or M4A. The source stays in its bucket untouched: each encoding or timeline job downloads its own copy and deletes the copy when it ends.

Think of the source as your master copy. Players never download it. They stream the [renditions](/docs/products/videos/renditions) encoded from it, which are smaller and split into short segments.

A few things follow from this:

- **The source cannot be replaced.** To stream a different file, create a new video. To fix a video, upload the corrected file and create a new video from it.
- **Upload the best quality you have.** Renditions can only be as good as the source. A 720p source makes a sharp 720p rendition but a soft 1080p one.
- **The source counts toward your Storage usage.** Keep it if you may encode more renditions later, since every new rendition reads it again.
- **The bucket's limits apply.** Its maximum file size and allowed extensions decide which sources you can upload. See [Buckets](/docs/products/storage/buckets#max-size).

Audio-only sources, such as a podcast episode, stream the same way as video, but have no timeline thumbnails.

# Create a video {% #create-a-video %}

In the Console, open **Videos** and click **Create video**. You can upload a new file, pick one from Storage, or import a demo movie. The Console also queues renditions for the profiles you select. See the [quick start](/docs/products/videos/quick-start).

With a server SDK, create the video and then its renditions:

{% multicode %}
  ```server-nodejs
  const sdk = require('node-appwrite');

  const client = new sdk.Client()
      .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
      .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>')
      .setKey('<API_KEY>');

  const videos = new sdk.Videos(client);

  const video = await videos.create({
      bucketId: '<BUCKET_ID>',
      fileId: '<FILE_ID>',
      name: 'Launch keynote' // optional, defaults to the file name
  });
  ```

  ```http
  POST /v1/videos HTTP/1.1
  Content-Type: application/json
  X-Appwrite-Project: <PROJECT_ID>
  X-Appwrite-Key: <API_KEY>

  {
    "bucketId": "<BUCKET_ID>",
    "fileId": "<FILE_ID>",
    "name": "Launch keynote"
  }
  ```
{% /multicode %}

Creating a video stores the document and queues [timeline](/docs/products/videos/timeline) generation. Nothing is encoded until you [create renditions](/docs/products/videos/renditions#create-renditions).

# Metadata {% #metadata %}

Media metadata is empty right after you create a video. The first rendition or timeline job probes the source and fills it in, so you never parse media files yourself. Because a timeline job is queued when you create the video, metadata arrives even before you encode anything.

| Field | Description |
|-------|-------------|
| `name` | Display name, up to 128 characters. Defaults to the source file name |
| `bucketId`, `fileId` | The source file in Storage |
| `size` | Source size in bytes |
| `format` | Container format, for example MP4 |
| `duration` | Duration in milliseconds |
| `width`, `height`, `aspectRatio` | Picture size in pixels, and its ratio such as `16:9` |
| `videoCodec`, `videoBitRate`, `videoFrameRate` | Video stream details. Bitrate is in bits per second |
| `audioCodec`, `audioBitRate`, `audioSampleRate` | Audio stream details. Empty when the source has no audio |
| `previewId` | The [preview image](/docs/products/videos/timeline#previews) used as the poster |

{% info title="Bitrate units" %}
Bitrates on a video are in **bits per second**. Bitrates on [profiles](/docs/products/videos/profiles) and [renditions](/docs/products/videos/renditions) are in **kilobits per second**.
{% /info %}

Metadata is useful before anything plays. Show the duration in a list, pick a layout from `aspectRatio` for portrait or landscape clips, or skip [profiles](/docs/products/videos/profiles#upscaling) taller than `height` so you never upscale.

To wait for metadata in your own code, poll the video until `duration` is set:

```js
async function waitForMetadata(videoId, { timeoutMs = 60000 } = {}) {
    const deadline = Date.now() + timeoutMs;
    while (Date.now() < deadline) {
        const video = await videos.get({ videoId });
        if (video.duration > 0) return video;
        await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 2000));
    }
    throw new Error('Metadata was not probed in time');
}
```

# Status {% #status %}

A video has no status of its own. The Console summarizes the status of its renditions:

| Status | Meaning |
|--------|---------|
| **Encoding** | At least one rendition is still processing |
| **Ready** | At least one rendition is ready to stream |
| **Failed** | Every rendition that finished failed or was aborted |
| **Not encoded** | The video has no renditions yet |

# List and search {% #list-and-search %}

List videos with [queries](/docs/products/databases/tablesdb/queries) and an optional `search` term. You can filter and sort on `bucketId`, `fileId`, `name`, `size`, `format`, `duration`, `width`, `height`, `videoCodec`, `videoBitRate`, `audioCodec`, and `audioBitRate`.

```js
const { Query } = sdk;

const { videos: longVideos } = await videos.list({
    queries: [
        Query.greaterThan('duration', 600000), // longer than 10 minutes
        Query.orderDesc('$createdAt')
    ]
});
```

# Connect videos to your data {% #connect-videos-to-your-data %}

A video holds media details, not app details. Keep titles, descriptions, owners, and visibility in your own [database](/docs/products/databases) table, and store the video ID in a column. Your app reads the row, then builds the [manifest URL](/docs/products/videos/outputs#master-manifest-urls) from the ID.

| Column | Example |
|--------|---------|
| `title` | `Launch keynote` |
| `videoId` | `6650f1c2a8b1` |
| `ownerId` | The user who uploaded it |
| `published` | `true` once renditions are ready |

# Automate with Functions {% #automate-with-functions %}

To turn every upload into a stream without opening the Console, create the video and its renditions from a [function](/docs/products/functions) that runs on the `buckets.<BUCKET_ID>.files.*.create` [event](/docs/products/functions/execute#events).

Large files upload in chunks, and the create event fires once per chunk. Act only when the upload is complete:

```js
import { Client, Videos } from 'node-appwrite';

// Profile IDs from Videos > Encoding profiles, stored as a function variable
const PROFILE_IDS = process.env.VIDEO_PROFILE_IDS.split(',');

export default async ({ req, res, log }) => {
    const file = req.body;

    // Skip partial chunks and anything that is not video or audio
    if (file.chunksUploaded < file.chunksTotal) return res.empty();
    if (!/^(video|audio)\//.test(file.mimeType)) return res.empty();

    const client = new Client()
        .setEndpoint(process.env.APPWRITE_FUNCTION_API_ENDPOINT)
        .setProject(process.env.APPWRITE_FUNCTION_PROJECT_ID)
        .setKey(req.headers['x-appwrite-key']);

    const videos = new Videos(client);

    const video = await videos.create({
        bucketId: file.bucketId,
        fileId: file.$id
    });

    for (const profileId of PROFILE_IDS) {
        await videos.createRendition({
            videoId: video.$id,
            profileId,
            output: 'cmaf'
        });
    }

    log(`Created video ${video.$id} from ${file.name}`);
    return res.json({ videoId: video.$id });
};
```

Give the function the `videos.write` scope in its settings so the key in `x-appwrite-key` can create videos. Use your videos bucket ID in the event rather than `buckets.*`, so the function ignores uploads to other buckets.

# Update and delete {% #update-and-delete %}

You can change a video's **name** at any time from **Settings** on the video page, or with `videos.update()`.

Deleting a video removes its renditions, captions, previews, and transcoded segments. The source file stays in Storage. Delete it from its bucket if you no longer need it.
